{"name":"Crimson Silk","hex":"#b5413b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"crimson","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A deep, slightly bluish red."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a deep red colour.","Immodest. (Can we add an example for this sense?)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crimson"},{"term":"silk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine fiber excreted by the silkworm or other arthropod (such as a spider).","A thread or yarn made from silk fibers."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To develop silk.","To remove the silk from (corn)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/silk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Crimson","blocks":[{"text":"Crimson is a rich, deep red color, inclining to purple. It originally meant the color of the kermes dye produced from a scale insect, Kermes vermilio, but the name is now sometimes also used as a generic term for slightly bluish-red colors that are between red and rose. It is the national color of Nepal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Maple_Leaf_Red_Ground_2000px.jpg/330px-Maple_Leaf_Red_Ground_2000px.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimson","wikidata":"Q303826"},{"title":"Silk","blocks":[{"text":"Silk is a natural protein fiber, some forms of which can be woven or knitted into textiles. The protein fiber of silk is composed mainly of fibroin (75-80%) and a smaller coating of sericin (20-25%). It is most commonly produced from the cocoons of certain insect larvae. The best-known silk is obtained from the cocoons of the larvae of the mulberry silkworm Bombyx mori, which are reared in captivity (sericulture)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Silk_raw_01a.jpg/330px-Silk_raw_01a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silk","wikidata":